f a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-12y+10{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=5{e}^{2t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+12y=5{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3y=10{e}^{t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3y=5{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+6y=10{e}^{t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+6y=5{e}^{2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+6y=5{e}^{2t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=12\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=6{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-12{y}^{′}-15y+6{e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=2{e}^{4t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=2{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y={e}^{4t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{5}y=6{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=6{e}^{4t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=2{e}^{4t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-15y+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+15y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+15y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+15y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+5y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+5y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ An external force $F(t)=9{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n6{y}^{″}=-18y+9{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+18y=6{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+3y={e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+6y=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{3}{2}y=3{e}^{-3t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+3y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-18y+12{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+4.5y=12{t}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+4.5y=6{t}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+9y=12{t}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+9y=3{t}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=10{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+10{e}^{t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =-1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+2$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $y={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a constant, we assume that the particular solution is also a constant,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nOnly II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-79586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Dev