 sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-2t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-79575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=8-16t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi /2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi }+1-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2{e}^{-\\pi }+2-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-79600" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=8{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $1$ second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(1)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3-1)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡1+3)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-2)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(1)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2+3)\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(1)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2+1)\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in inches, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion with light damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $34\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =8.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=-2,\\,Q=-9.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-{e}^{-t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-{e}^{-\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(\\pi )=(1+3{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=(3-{e}^{-2\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=(4-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=2(1+2{e}^{-2\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "y(\\pi )=2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\pi )=2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2},\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{cm}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-94304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3457650e851f66d12098aae9a11e7664e71dc69d17cb46d21ce0946ed39555ce"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1-\\pi )\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(3+\\pi )\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "q-64643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5c7977f8acd2ffcab4719e785af9e89bb1db28b98c19498eaa167529a784d21"} {"question" "e-6731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0.$ Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = t$\n\n- $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n\n- $y''+y'+3y = 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6731.json" "file_sha256" "7cf186f226028ed6a709aa690eac86b54b97571e6943bbaaa5994588bb91d9e4"} {"question" "e-6731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$\n\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0.$\n\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6731.json" "file_sha256" "7cf186f226028ed6a709aa690eac86b54b97571e6943bbaaa5994588bb91d9e4"} {"question" "e-6732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6732.json" "file_sha256" "5e10db39e5c040e140cab42b1a266daa034728979cf3f26c81998a5419de4c68"} {"question" "e-6732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6732.json" "file_sha256" "5e10db39e5c040e140cab42b1a266daa034728979cf3f26c81998a5419de4c68"} {"question" "e-6733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 50t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n\n- $y = R e ^ {- t} \\cos \\left( 2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n\n- $y =e^{-t}\\left( A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6733.json" "file_sha256" "11ed26f4beba67672eb305f2d2bfd9d8b988bc25f25e17c113c0988df5bf3228"} {"question" "e-6733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation $y''+2y' + 5 y = 0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t)=50t$ is a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+10t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6733.json" "file_sha256" "11ed26f4beba67672eb305f2d2bfd9d8b988bc25f25e17c113c0988df5bf3228"} {"question" "e-6734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6734.json" "file_sha256" "5fad38c348143f5404970eff082170b7256f98d50d885a87114a772cec5b046f"} {"question" "e-6734" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0.$ So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1,\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t).$ Note that the external force $f(t) = 2e^{-t}$ is an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha  e^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. \n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1.$ Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1,$ and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1.$\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/example-6734.json" "file_sha256" "5fad38c348143f5404970eff082170b7256f98d50d885a87114a772cec5b046f"} {"question" "q-64646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(\\pi )=2(1-{e}^{-\\pi })\\,\\text{in}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065932/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "874f3fd64d932049df99fe72819a9206351142a152f7b8b42a23743a943c5e4f" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "A particular solution is 2cos(2t)+8sin(2t). The complementary solution is e^(−t)[Pcos(2t)+Qsin(2t)]. Initial conditions y(0)=y'(0)=0 give P=−2 and Q=−9. At t=π, cos(2π)=1 and sin(2π)=0, yielding y(π)=2−2e^(−π) inches. Original option c (index 2), the authentic worked solution, and successful grading agree. The stored trailing in denotes inches